---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// Facts about how this site is built and deployed. Keep them in step with
// .github/workflows/deploy.yml, .npmrc and infra/settings.tf.
const repo = 'teruokun/codeecologist.com';
const repoUrl = `https://github.com/${repo}`;
const site = 'https://codeecologist.com';
const scorecardUrl = `https://scorecard.dev/viewer/?uri=github.com/${repo}`;
---

<Base
  title="Supply chain"
  description="How this site is built, what it depends on, and how to verify that what it serves is what was built."
>
  <div class="page prose">
    <h1>This site's supply chain</h1>
    <p>
      This site is a small, static website. It is built and deployed in the open from
      {' '}<a href={repoUrl}>{repo}</a>. This page lists what goes into it and how you can check that
      the files you are served are the files that were built.
    </p>

    <h2>What's pinned, and how</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>npm dependencies</strong> are pinned to exact versions, installed only from the
        committed lockfile (<code>npm ci</code>), with install scripts turned off.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Node.js</strong> is pinned to one version in <code>.node-version</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub Actions</strong> are pinned to full commit SHAs, not tags. Every workflow
        starts with no token permissions, and each job is granted only what it needs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deploy tools</strong> (Cloudflare's <code>wrangler</code>) have their own lockfile
        and are installed only in the deploy job, so they are never part of the build.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Updates</strong> come from Dependabot once a week. A new release waits 7 days before
        it is proposed, and every update is reviewed and merged by hand.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No third-party scripts or requests.</strong> The page loads nothing from any other
        site, and the Content-Security-Policy enforces that.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>SBOM</h2>
    <p>
      Each deploy publishes a CycloneDX software bill of materials for the build, including build
      tools: <a href="/.well-known/sbom.cdx.json">/.well-known/sbom.cdx.json</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Checksums and provenance</h2>
    <p>
      The build writes the SHA-256 of every file it produces to
      {' '}<a href="/.well-known/site-checksums.txt">/.well-known/site-checksums.txt</a>. GitHub Actions
      then signs a <a href="https://slsa.dev/">SLSA</a> build provenance attestation for each of
      those files, and an attestation for the SBOM. The deploy job verifies the attestation and
      every checksum before anything is uploaded.
    </p>

    <h2>Verify it yourself</h2>
    <p>
      With the <a href="https://cli.github.com/">GitHub CLI</a> installed:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`# The checksums file: built from this repository by the deploy workflow
curl -sO ${site}/.well-known/site-checksums.txt
gh attestation verify site-checksums.txt -R ${repo}

# Any single page, for example the homepage
curl -s ${site}/ -o index.html
gh attestation verify index.html -R ${repo}

# The SBOM attestation
gh attestation verify site-checksums.txt -R ${repo} \\
  --predicate-type https://cyclonedx.org/bom`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      To pin the exact workflow that signed it, add
      {' '}<code>--signer-workflow {repo}/.github/workflows/deploy.yml</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>OpenSSF Scorecard</h2>
    <p>
      The repository is scored weekly by the
      {' '}<a href={scorecardUrl}>OpenSSF Scorecard</a>, which checks for practices such as pinned
      dependencies, branch protection and token permissions.
    </p>

    <h2>Why some Cloudflare features are off</h2>
    <p>
      Cloudflare can rewrite pages as it serves them: obfuscating email addresses, deferring
      scripts (Rocket Loader) or injecting analytics. Each of those would change the bytes after
      they were built and attested, so all three are turned off, and that setting is managed in
      code alongside the DNS.
    </p>

    <h2>Known limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Cloudflare serves the files, so you still trust Cloudflare to serve the bytes it was given.
        The attestations let you detect a difference, not prevent one.
      </li>
      <li>
        URLs that end in <code>/</code> are served from an <code>index.html</code> file; save the
        page under that name, as in the example above, before verifying it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Response headers (such as the Content-Security-Policy) come from the
        {' '}<code>_headers</code> file, which Cloudflare reads as configuration rather than serving.
        It is covered by the checksums, but headers that Cloudflare adds at the edge are not.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
